{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SSGSu9TyrZkR4yYxzrKUAQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tuse std::{ffi::c_void, fmt::Debug, ptr};\n2\t\n3\tuse crate::{\n4\t    copy::MemCopyD2H,\n5\t    error::{check, CudaError},\n6\t    memory_manager::{d_free, d_malloc},\n7\t    stream::{cudaStreamPerThread, cudaStream_t},\n8\t};\n9\t\n10\t#[link(name = \"cudart\")]\n11\textern \"C\" {\n12\t    pub fn cudaMemsetAsync(dst: *mut c_void, value: i32, count: usize, stream: cudaStream_t)\n13\t        -> i32;\n14\t}\n15\t\n16\t/// Struct that owns a buffer allocated on GPU device. The struct only holds the raw pointer and\n17\t/// length, but this struct has a `Drop` implementation which frees the associated device memory.\n18\t#[repr(C)]\n19\tpub struct DeviceBuffer<T> {\n20\t    ptr: *mut T,\n21\t    len: usize,\n22\t}\n23\t\n24\t/// A struct that packs a pointer with a size in bytes to pass on CUDA.\n25\t/// It holds `*const c_void` for being a universal simple type that can be read by CUDA.\n26\t/// Since it is hard to enforce immutability preservation, it just holds `*const`,\n27\t/// but has two separate constructors for more robustness from the usage perspective.\n28\t/// This is essentially a [DeviceBuffer] but without owning the data.\n29\t#[repr(C)]\n30\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone)]\n31\tpub struct DeviceBufferView {\n32\t    pub ptr: *const c_void,\n33\t    pub size: usize,\n34\t}\n35\t\n36\tunsafe impl<T> Send for DeviceBuffer<T> {}\n37\tunsafe impl<T> Sync for DeviceBuffer<T> {}\n38\t\n39\timpl<T> DeviceBuffer<T> {\n40\t    /// Creates an \"empty\" DeviceBuffer with a null pointer and zero length.\n41\t    #[allow(clippy::new_without_default)]\n42\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n43\t        DeviceBuffer {\n44\t            ptr: ptr::null_mut(),\n45\t            len: 0,\n46\t        }\n47\t    }\n48\t\n49\t    /// # Safety\n50\t    /// - The caller must ensure that the pointer `ptr` is valid for `len` elements of type `T` in\n51\t    