ying buffers and store metadata like offsets and dimensions separately. Then when I need to call sumcheck_polys_batch_eval, I can reconstruct lightweight DeviceMatrix views from that metadata. This way the fold logic operates on raw buffers without ownership complications, and I only create temporary views when needed for the sumcheck evaluation. from buf_a into buf_b, then swap them and reconstruct fresh views pointing into the new current buffer for the next round's sumcheck evaluation. The key insight is that I don't need to keep old views alive—I can just update the metadata and regenerate views as needed. Actually, the simplest fix might be to let DeviceBuffer handle view reconstruction internally so I'm not manually managing view lifetimes across rounds.\n\nWait, I'm overcomplicating this. The real blocker is that DeviceBuffer doesn't track whether it owns its memory, so I can't safely create non-owning views. If I just add a flag to DeviceBuffer to mark non-owning instances, then the Drop impl can skip deallocation for those, and I can freely create and drop views without leaks or double-frees. That's the one change that unblocks everything else.\n\nImplementing DeviceBuffer modifications...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12868,"cache_read_input_tokens":75271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12868},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2c41f48-5b80-4de8-b307-355a7b8c0ec2","uuid":"dfc887af-c559-44a6-a1bc-3814a427b993"}
